MODIFICATION NOTICE
-------------------
These sources are a DERIVED WORK of the pin above, not a copy of it. In October
2026 the 24 Java files were translated into Kotlin so that the handshake can run
on Apple platforms as well as on Android. The translation keeps the protocol,
the state machine, the message layouts and the upstream comments; a byte for
byte diff against the pin is therefore no longer possible, and `src/commonTest`
exists in its place. Those tests hold values recorded from the original Java and
BouncyCastle code, including a whole four digit pairing replayed message by
message, and they run on the JVM, on Android and on the iOS simulator.

What the translation replaced, and why:
  - BouncyCastle (secp256r1 point arithmetic, ECDSA, ASN.1 key readers) and
    java.math.BigInteger have no multiplatform form. They are replaced by
    jamorham.keks.crypto: KeksUInt, KeksP256 / KeksEcPoint, KeksEcdsa, KeksDer.
  - Project Lombok (@Getter, @val, @RequiredArgsConstructor) is not needed in
    Kotlin and is gone, with the annotation processor.
  - javax.crypto, java.security.SecureRandom and android.util.Log are now three
    `expect` declarations with an actual per platform.
  - java.nio.ByteBuffer is replaced by jamorham.keks.util.KeksBuffer, which
    copies the three behaviours the message classes depend on.

